===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|lang=gmh|brot|brōt}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|brot|brōt|lang=goh}} (attested since the 8th century), from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{recons|braudan|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{recons|bʰrew-||to seethe, to boil|lang=ine-pro}}; an extension of {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{recons|bʰerə-||to well up, to boil, to be in motion (as of fire or water)|lang=ine-pro}}. Originating from the same root are {{term|Brühe|lang=de}}, {{term|Brei|lang=de}}, {{term|braten|lang=de}} and {{term|brennen|lang=de}}.

''Germanic Cognates'' with identical meaning include Old Saxon {{term|brod|brōd|lang=osx}} (> Low German {{term|Broot|lang=nds}}, {{term|Brot|lang=nds}}), Old Dutch ''*brōd'' (> Dutch {{term|brood|lang=nl}}, Afrikaans {{term|brood|lang=af}}), Old English {{term|bread|brēad|lang=ang}} (> Modern English {{term|bread|lang=en}}, Scots {{term|breid|lang=sco}}), West Frisian {{term|brea|lang=fy}} and Old Norse {{term|brauð|lang=non}} (> Icelandic {{term|brauð|lang=is}}, Faroese {{term|breyð|lang=fo}}, Norwegian {{term|brød|lang=no}}, Swedish {{term|bröd|lang=sv}}, Danish {{term|brød|lang=da}}).

''Non-Germanic-Cognates'' include Ancient Greek {{term|sc=polytonic|βρύτος|tr=brutos||fermented barley beverage|lang=grc}}, Latin {{term|ferveo||to be hot, to boil, to burn|lang=la}}, {{term|fermentum||fermentation, leaven|lang=la}}, Albanian {{term|mbruaj||to knead|lang=sq}}, Old Armenian {{term|բորբ|sc=Armn|tr=borb||bright, aflame|lang=xcl}}. Originally, the meaning of ''Brot'' was ''"what has been fermented, leaven"'' and may be a nominal derivative from {{etyl|gem-pro|-}} {{recons|brewwanan||to brew|lang=gem-pro}} (> German {{term|brauen|lang=de}}). It replaced the older {{term|Laib||loaf|lang=de}} which was the more common term in Old High German (cf. the use of {{term|hlaf|hlāf|lang=ang}} and {{term|bread|brēad|lang=ang}} in Old English).<ref>Pfeifer, Wolfgang. 1995, 2005. ''Etymologisches Wörterbuch des Deutschen.'' München: dtv. ISBN 3423325119.</ref>

Alternative etymologies derive ''Brot'' and its cognates from {{etyl|gem-pro|-}} {{recons|braudaz|lang=gem-pro}}, {{recons|brauþaz||broken piece, fragment|lang=gem-pro}} and from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{recons|bʰera-||to split, beat, hew, struggle|lang=ine-pro}} (cf. Swedish {{term|bryta||to break|lang=sv}}, Albanian {{term|brydh||weak, mild|lang=sq}}, English {{term|brittle|lang=sv}}). A conflation of both roots could also be possible.

